## 1. The two licences, and the one sentence that decides each

| what | licence | why that one |
|---|---|---|
| rlxfw's own **kernel** files | `GPL-2.0-only` | forced by provenance: they are compiled into, and linked with, a kernel whose own `COPYING` says version 2 and not "or later" (§ 2) |
| rlxfw's own **userspace** programs, **tools**, scripts, declarations, web assets and documents | `MIT` | chosen. MIT is compatible in both directions with the two licences the image combines it with — statically linked LGPL-2.1 uClibc and GPL-2.0 kernel headers — so the image carries no compatibility question (§ 4) |

**What `LICENSE` holds.** `LICENSE` is the MIT text and nothing else, so that a
tool matching licence texts can read it as MIT. 量 2026-10-05 (`gh api`, before
`33cb73a7`) GitHub's licence detection read it as `NOASSERTION`, and 量
2026-10-08 (`gh api`) it reads `license.spdx_id` `MIT`; 讀 the only change to
`LICENSE` between the two reads is `33cb73a7`'s removal of the paragraph it
then ended with, so 推 that paragraph was why, as this file inferred on
2026-10-05 — GitHub's detector may also have changed, and nothing here reads
it. The paragraph moved here verbatim on 2026-10-05; in it, *this file* means
`LICENSE`:

> This file covers rlxfw's own userspace programs, tools and documents. It does
> NOT cover rlxfw's own kernel files, which are GPL-2.0-only, nor any third
> party's code in this repository or in the image rlxfw builds. `NOTICE` names
> every clause and the files it covers; `docs/sbom.md` is the component list.

**Why not one licence for both halves.** 讀 GPL-2.0 § 2(b): a work derived from
a GPL-2.0 program, distributed as a whole, is licensed under that licence.
rlxfw's kernel files are not a separate work that happens to sit beside the
kernel; they are compiled against its headers, they call its functions, and the
linker puts them in the same `vmlinux` (§ 2). The userspace programs are not:
they reach the kernel through system calls, and the kernel's own `COPYING` says
in so many words that this does not make them derived works 讀. So the two
halves are two different provenance arguments, and writing one licence over
both would hide that.

**Why not Apache-2.0, which the project plan named.** `plan/` item `D10` said
*kernel GPLv2, userspace Apache-2.0*. It is **stale** on two counts and was not
followed: it names uClibc-ng and monocypher, and 量 the image has uClibc 0.9.30
(§ 5) and TweetNaCl (§ 6) instead. On the licence itself, 推: Apache-2.0's
patent-termination and patent-grant clauses are conventionally read as
additional restrictions that GPL-2.0 § 6 does not permit, so an Apache-2.0
userspace combined with GPL-2.0 kernel code and LGPL-2.1 library code raises a
question that MIT does not raise at all. That reading is the common one and it
is not this file's to settle; MIT was chosen so that nothing turns on it.

